What did we actually learn?
If it was pushed to GitHub before catching it:
Immediately revoke/rotate the AWS credentials.
Then rewrite Git history with git-filter-repo.
Force-push the cleaned history.
Check for other copies/caches if necessary.

Why can it still be compromised after rewriting?

Because someone may already have:

old clone
old commit
fork
downloaded copy
CI/build logs

Rewriting GitHub doesn't magically erase copies that other people already obtained.

Actual response when a secret leaks:

Rotate/revoke the secret first. Then clean the Git history.

History cleanup is important, but the leaked credential must be considered compromised.

Your assignment's core lesson
Secret committed
      ↓
Delete file ❌
      ↓
Secret STILL in Git history
      ↓
Rewrite history with filter-repo
      ↓
Secret removed from rewritten history ✅
      ↓
BUT...
      ↓
Old copies may still exist
      ↓
ROTATE/REVOKE THE SECRET 🔐

And one correction to keep in mind: the AWS-looking values in this exercise are example credentials, not real ones.
